WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the&nbsp;
program today, David Sirota, who is a journalist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.920
columnist and founder and editor of The Lever,&nbsp;
which you can find at Lever News.com. It's been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:15.109
little while since since we had you on and you've&nbsp;
been busy, but it's really great to have you on.&nbsp;

00:00:15.109 --> 00:00:17.329
Speaker 3: Thanks. Thanks.&nbsp;
Thanks so much for having me.&nbsp;

00:00:17.329 --> 00:00:22.380
Speaker 1: So, I mean, let's just kind of&nbsp;
start start at the top here. What was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:28.740
goal with creating the lever? What are the&nbsp;
issues in the media landscape that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:34.560
hoping to solve or improve upon or address?
Speaker 3: Sure. I mean, I had been working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.740
for Bernie Sanders as his speechwriter on the&nbsp;
2020 presidential campaign, but before that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:47.160
I spent many years as a journalist. And after&nbsp;
the campaign, I decided to do some writing. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:52.860
you know, I'd actually started out as a as just&nbsp;
a small substack, but there was a lot of reader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:01:00.120
support for it. And so I teamed up with a former&nbsp;
reporter that I had formerly worked with, Andrew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:09.000
Perez, to start doing some actual reporting.&nbsp;
And from there, it kind of grew. And and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:16.080
look, I think that what what our focus is, what&nbsp;
a lever story is, is a story that's about. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:25.200
about following the money. It's about following&nbsp;
the power. Our our mission is to actually change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:32.040
the conversation and change the policies in this&nbsp;
country so we don't pretend to just be, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:38.880
reporting whatever is going on. We our reporting&nbsp;
is accountability journalism designed to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.960
politicians accountable when it comes to issues&nbsp;
that deal with money and power. So, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:52.920
in the last month or so, we broke open the story&nbsp;
of the deregulatory decisions that were made on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:59.520
rail safety in the lead up to the East Palestine,&nbsp;
Ohio derailment. We this week we spent a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.820
time breaking open stories about the regulatory&nbsp;
decisions that were made to deregulate Silicon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:13.020
Valley Bank and what were the decisions made&nbsp;
by politicians for which donors. So we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:18.360
constantly asking the question, who are public&nbsp;
officials actually serving? We are constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:25.020
spotlighting that. So with the idea that the&nbsp;
more that the readership knows, the more that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:31.500
the readership can be engaged to actually pressure&nbsp;
their lawmakers to do to make better decisions.&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:37.620
Speaker 1: So how is this funded? Because one&nbsp;
of the interesting things is, particularly when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.920
the focus is to follow the money and to have&nbsp;
accountability. My guess is you have to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.349
particularly careful about following the money&nbsp;
when it comes to your organization as well.&nbsp;

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:55.200
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, we're look, we're reader&nbsp;
supported. We work with other media outlets like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:02.340
Jacobin as an example, syndicates our work some&nbsp;
some of our work appears in The Guardian. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:10.620
so we have a kind of I mean, we're small, right?&nbsp;
So we're trying to to build out. But we we are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:18.060
reader supported news organization that holds them&nbsp;
accountable, holds them being the proverbial them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:26.340
meaning corporations, politicians and the like.&nbsp;
And I think that what you'll see is, is that on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.920
our stories, one thing that I'm really proud of on&nbsp;
our stories is I think when you read our stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:38.280
you don't have to trust us, right? Because what we&nbsp;
do in every story and we take a lot of time to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:46.260
this is to link to all of the primary sources in&nbsp;
which we are referring to. So I say this all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.160
time. If you read one of our stories is all you&nbsp;
know. Sirota is this Sirota, is that, hey, listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.460
that's fine. You can believe whatever you want&nbsp;
about me. The links to the source materials are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:03.420
all there for you to evaluate. I fully believe in&nbsp;
in. I don't even believe in trust, but verify. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:09.300
believe first and foremost in verify. So when you&nbsp;
read one of our stories, when you see it linked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.980
to all the that source material, that's because&nbsp;
we're trying to show the reader, hey, listen, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.240
you don't trust us about a statement, an assertion&nbsp;
that we make here, a statement of fact that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:24.660
make your go, makes you can go fact check us.&nbsp;
And we have a vigorous fact checking process in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:32.400
what we do. But that's the point here, is that I&nbsp;
think that we a lot of ways we in media, the media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:39.540
ecosystem has gotten away from that which has&nbsp;
created an environment in which people can say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:45.420
oh, that's, you know, quote unquote, fake news.&nbsp;
So the way we combat that is to try to present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:51.600
all of our facts and be very open and transparent&nbsp;
about that so you can debate, you know, this or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.329
that about the writer or about the publication,&nbsp;
but you can't debate the documents and facts.&nbsp;

00:04:56.329 --> 00:05:01.440
Speaker 1: What are your thoughts on whether&nbsp;
there can be other. There are funding methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.380
for organizations like yours or mine or larger&nbsp;
media or whatever that you find journalistically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:14.400
ethical or kosher or whatever word you might&nbsp;
want to apply to it in the sense of if there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:20.220
any kind of advertising. Is that necessarily&nbsp;
a problem or does it depend on the subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:24.240
matter that is being covered and the types of&nbsp;
products and services being advertised or like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.740
what other models are okay in your mind?
Speaker 3: It's a great question. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:38.640
to my mind I am look, I am we don't have corporate&nbsp;
ads, right, and the like, but I'm not a purist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.620
when it comes to this. I'm not a purist when it&nbsp;
comes to foundation funding. I'm not a purist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:52.980
when it comes to, you know, individual donors. I&nbsp;
think that we live in a world of unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.980 --> 00:06:01.860
where getting resources to do public interest&nbsp;
journalism. There is no public funding for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:06.840
that in any kind of institutional level.&nbsp;
There is in a sense of crowdsourcing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:13.740
but that is a very, very tough, tough road. It's&nbsp;
a worthwhile road. We are on that road as a as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.680
as an entity. So it's hard for me to create, you&nbsp;
know, hard and fast rules other than to say this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:31.620
I think being reader supported at some capacity is&nbsp;
important, and having that be a major pillar of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:39.120
media organization is extremely important for many&nbsp;
reasons, one of which is you want to be serving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:47.880
a readership and at least have that be a a huge&nbsp;
pillar of what you are, who you are answering to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:55.980
for the resources to do journalism. I also think&nbsp;
that that that having reader support is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:02.700
over the long haul, if you can do it, is actually&nbsp;
more stable in the sense of a lot of small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:12.000
comparatively small contributors becomes a more&nbsp;
stable funding base than one or two or three huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.740
funders or huge corporations that can wake up on&nbsp;
a on a given day and just pull funding. Right. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.520
I think there's a stability issue there. Yeah.
Speaker 1: One of the things about that that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.600
interesting, which I've talked with my audience&nbsp;
