00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.589
but we also know that the appetite for nuance is&nbsp;
maybe more limited than we would like it to be.&nbsp;

00:13:45.589 --> 00:13:50.160
Speaker 3: That's a great that's a really,&nbsp;
really great question. Well, first and foremost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:57.540
I think we have to understand that there is a&nbsp;
learning process that as we learn new information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:05.280
conclusions will change. I mean, that's that's&nbsp;
part of the human experience. Anybody who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:11.520
who doesn't want to learn new information and&nbsp;
thus change conclusions is I mean, you're heading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:17.580
straight into denialism, right? So I don't think&nbsp;
it's I don't think media should participate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:26.280
any kind of denialism at all. I think I think&nbsp;
the other point about this is to is actually to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:33.840
what we try to do in our reporting. I always say&nbsp;
this to my reporters is to state what we know as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:41.520
fact right now. Yeah, don't foreclose on the idea&nbsp;
that the facts in the future can change, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:49.680
don't say things that are not grounded in facts&nbsp;
that are that can't be verified. So, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:58.800
did ex lead to why did this deregulation lead&nbsp;
to this disaster? You can only really say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:07.440
if you can prove it, but you can say. These&nbsp;
senators or these lawmakers ignored warnings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:16.080
about this outcome to cast votes for that kind of&nbsp;
deregulation. And then this happened right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:23.760
So my point is, is that that's a nuance, but it's&nbsp;
an important point. As long as you're still saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:31.020
what can be factually verified, I think that's&nbsp;
the obligation. And I mean, that is the job of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:38.160
journalism, is to say what we know to be true&nbsp;
without speculating or extrapolating. It's when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:44.220
you get into extrapolating ahead in the future&nbsp;
that it becomes problematic, Then it can become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:48.840
I mean, I hate this term, but then it can become&nbsp;
too susceptible to the charge of quote unquote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:55.560
fake news. Yeah, nothing is fake news if you're&nbsp;
stating in this document or on this day this thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.560 --> 00:16:00.900
happened and you can pretend it didn't happened.&nbsp;
But that's like pretending the sky isn't blue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:04.920
That's, to me, the. The role of the journalist.
